title the wrap that hid a citation
prompt make sure the prompt is sent
think The prompt IS sent -- 5,717 bytes on both doors, tracked and committed. Verifying it is what found the fault: I checked the path as I had written it in the commit body, and nothing was there.
think The real stamp is 183107 and I wrote 183418, typed from memory in the one place a citation is permanent. Rule four of the commit hook exists for exactly that and welcomed it.
think The mechanism is the wrap. The hook splits the body on spaces and needs a slash and an extension in one token, and my line broke between them -- expanding-prompts/ with no extension, then the basename with no slash. Two harmless halves where a path had been.
obs Nothing here was careless, which is what makes it worth booking. The body was wrapped to the tree's own margin, and the formatting is what hid the fault. A rule enforced per line inherits the line's arbitrary boundaries.
obs Fixed by resolving a bare one-clock basename against git ls-files, since the naming law makes such a basename globally unique. Proven on the exact citation that escaped, both directions.
obs Named the limit rather than implying the hole is closed: a path wrapped mid-directory with no dated basename in it stays invisible.
loom commit_guard cases_before=25 cases_after=28 wrapped_refused=yes wrapped_real_welcomed=yes
file tools/hooks/commit-msg a bare dated basename is resolved even when the wrap ate its directory
file construction/REDS.md %437 -- a line break walked a fabricated citation past the guard written for it
recommend check-in Keaton -- the handoff stands at 20260905-183107 on both doors; the printout line is corrected in the reply
